  "properties": {
    "encrypted": "false",
    "long_instructions": "## Dropdown Category Search\r\n\r\nYour goal is to finish and test a **dropdown-based search** that helps students filter study resources by **subject** (like Math, Biology, US History, etc). The function should:\r\n\r\n- Take `data`, `field`, and `selected` (from the dropdown).\r\n- Trim extra spaces from the selected value.\r\n- Compare values in a **case-insensitive** way.\r\n- Show **all resources** when `\"All\"` is selected.\r\n- Skip resources that are missing the target field.\r\n- Not change the original dataset.\r\n\r\n::: details [Steps]\r\n\r\n1. In `script.js`, find the `searchByCategory(data, field, selected)` function.\r\n\r\n2. Use the AI helper (CHAT Co-Build):\r\n   - Paste just this function into the chat.\r\n   - Use the C–H–A–T structure:\r\n     - **Clarify**: Ask AI to restate what the function should do with `data`, `field`, and `selected`, and how it affects what shows in the results area (the list of study resources and match counts).\r\n     - **Hone**: Ask for help adding:\r\n       - `trim()` to clean the selected value.\r\n       - Lowercasing on both sides before comparing.\r\n       - A guard so resources missing `item[field]` are skipped safely.\r\n       - A check that if `\"All\"` is selected, all resources are returned.\r\n\r\n3. **Apply** Implement or refine the function based on the AI’s suggestions:\r\n   - Build a `results` array.\r\n   - Normalize the selected category.\r\n   - If the selected category is `\"All\"`, return all resources.\r\n   - Otherwise, return only the matching ones.\r\n\r\n4. Test Cases for the Study Hub:\r\n   - **Mixed case**: try selecting “math” vs “Math” — results should match either way.\r\n   - **Extra spaces**: simulate selecting an option with spaces before or after (e.g., `\"  Biology  \"`).\r\n   - **All option**: select “All” and make sure it shows all resources again.\r\n   - **Empty results**: if a subject option is added that doesn’t match any items, the app should show 0 matches and a message that says “No resources found.”\r\n\r\n5. In the backpack, save a reusable prompt **Template** you might use in the future, with placeholders like `{data}`, `{field}`, `{selected}` for dropdown-based study searches (e.g., `{subject}`, `{level}`, `{format}`).\r\n\r\n:::",
